Subject: arm/rp23xx: acknowledge only the owned DMA channel interrupt

INTS0 is write-one-to-clear, not an ordinary read/write register. RP23xx
setbits_reg32 and clrbits_reg32 use hardware atomic alias addresses, not a
software read-modify-write. Avoid relying on alias handling for this status
register: the Pico SDK acknowledges DMA interrupts through the ordinary
register address, and its RP2040 issue #974 reports lost completions through
atomic aliases. That report is not evidence of RP2350 hardware causality.

Write the owned channel mask directly, matching the documented normal-write
acknowledgement. Do not change interrupt routing,
callback ordering, transfer setup or DMA abort behavior.

diff --git a/arch/arm/src/rp23xx/rp23xx_dmac.c b/arch/arm/src/rp23xx/rp23xx_dmac.c
--- a/arch/arm/src/rp23xx/rp23xx_dmac.c
+++ b/arch/arm/src/rp23xx/rp23xx_dmac.c
@@ -251,7 +251,9 @@ DMA_HANDLE rp23xx_dmachannel(void)
 
   nxmutex_unlock(&g_dmac.lock);
 
-  setbits_reg32(bit, RP23XX_DMA_INTS0);
+  /* INTS0 is write-one-to-clear: preserve other channels' completions. */
+
+  putreg32(bit, RP23XX_DMA_INTS0);
   setbits_reg32(bit, RP23XX_DMA_INTE0);
 
   /* Since we have reserved a DMA descriptor by taking a count from chansem,
@@ -295,7 +297,7 @@ void rp23xx_dmafree(DMA_HANDLE handle)
   setbits_reg32(1 << dmach->chan, RP23XX_DMA_CHAN_ABORT);
   putreg32(0, RP23XX_DMA_CTRL_TRIG(ch));
   clrbits_reg32(1 << dmach->chan, RP23XX_DMA_INTE0);
-  clrbits_reg32(1 << dmach->chan, RP23XX_DMA_INTS0);
+  putreg32(1 << dmach->chan, RP23XX_DMA_INTS0);
 
   /* Mark the channel no longer in use.  Clearing the in-use flag is an
    * atomic operation and so should be safe.
